## The Only Difference That Actually Matters (It Lives at the Tip)

Both shapes taper. That is not the difference. Both narrow from a wider base toward the free edge, both create finger elongation, and both are filed using versions of the same technique. The entire difference between <strong>coffin nails vs almond nails</strong> lives at the final step of the filing sequence. What happens at the tip.

Almond stops at a soft, rounded point. The taper continues smoothly to a gentle peak, the way an actual almond nut narrows. The result is a continuous line, no interruption, no geometry imposed. Coffin continues that same taper through the sides, and then files the tip flat. That horizontal cut across the free edge is everything. It is what produces the coffin silhouette (or the ballerina slipper, which is the same shape with a more palatable name). It is also what introduces the corners.

Those corners are the structural weak point that determines durability. They are what catches on keyboards. They are what lifts on press-on sets when the sizing is slightly off. They are why the same set that looks incredible at week one can feel fragile by week two.

The rounded tip on almond distributes impact around a curve. There is no single stress concentration point. Pressure spreads. On coffin, the squared corners take direct lateral pressure every time the nail contacts a surface edge-first. According to <a href='https://www.nailsmag.com' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>NAILS Magazine nail shape guidance</a>, apex placement and stress point management are the primary structural considerations when choosing between tapered and flat-tip shapes.

Everything else in this comparison(flattery, durability, design compatibility, lifestyle fit) flows from that one distinction at the tip.

