## Which Shape Makes Your Fingers Look Longer, and When That Logic Breaks Down

Almond elongates more reliably. The continuous taper creates an unbroken line from cuticle to tip, drawing the eye outward without interruption. The visual flow never stops. On coffin, the taper does the same elongating work through the sides, but the flat tip introduces a horizontal line that acts as a visual full stop. The eye reaches the tip and pauses. On narrow or long fingers that pause reads as architectural and deliberate. On shorter or wider fingers, it can read as width.

This is where most comparison articles get contradictory, and why readers walk away more confused than when they arrived. You will find sources that say coffin suits wide nail beds, and sources that say almond suits wide nail beds, often within the same article. The nuance that gets lost: coffin can look good on wide nail beds at longer lengths, when the extended taper narrows the silhouette enough before the flat tip arrives. At shorter lengths, that taper does not have enough distance to do its work, and the flat tip can emphasise width rather than correct for it.

Almond does the corrective work at any length. Even a short almond retains the gentle taper that visually slims and extends. If your nail beds run wide, almond's tapering sidewalls do the work coffin simply cannot guarantee across lengths.

For shorter fingers specifically, almond wins by a wider margin. The rounded peak draws the eye forward and upward in a continuous motion. Coffin creates length through the body of the nail, but the flat tip interrupts the illusion just before it completes.

