## Which Shape Is More Durable, and Where Each One Fails

Almond is the more durable shape. The rounded tip distributes impact around a curve, spreading pressure rather than concentrating it at a point. There is no corner to catch, no edge to lift, no stress concentration point that bears the full force of a knock or a key.

Coffin's failure point is predictable and specific: the corners. Every time a coffin nail contacts a surface at an angle (a keyboard edge, a bag strap, the inside of a pocket) and the corner takes the full lateral pressure. According to <a href='https://www.nailpro.com' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Nailpro professional filing guidance</a>, coffin corners are the primary breakage zone in tapered flat-tip shapes, and reinforcing the apex during a gel or acrylic build is the standard professional response to this structural vulnerability.

This does not mean coffin cannot be durable. A properly built coffin set with good apex placement and adequate thickness at the stress points will outlast a poorly built almond. But all else being equal, almond has an inherent structural advantage. There is simply less to go wrong.

For press-on wearers, durability takes on a second dimension: fit. A coffin press-on that does not match your nail bed's curvature and sidewall width will have floating corners, and floating corners lift, catch, and pop. Most "my coffin press-ons keep breaking" complaints are not about the shape's structural weakness. They are about a set that was not sized precisely enough for the wearer's nail bed. Almond press-ons are more forgiving of slight width variation because the tapering tip accommodates small fit discrepancies without creating visible gaps.

