## Which Nail Shape Suits Your Hand Type? (A Practical Guide, Not a Pinterest Myth)

Skip the vague hand-type categories. Here is the practical breakdown.

**Wide nail beds.** Almond. The bilateral taper visually narrows the nail from both sides, creating a slimmer line. Coffin also tapers, but the flat tip reintroduces horizontal width at the end. If your nail beds are naturally wide, almond will consistently produce a more elongated, streamlined result. According to the <a href='https://www.lorealparisusa.com/beauty-magazine/beauty-tips/beauty-trends/nail-shapes' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>L'Oréal Paris nail shape guide</a>, tapered shapes with a pointed finish are the most effective at creating a slimming optical effect on wider nail beds.

**Short fingers.** Almond. The continuous taper draws the eye outward without interruption. Coffin creates length through the body of the nail but breaks the illusion at the flat tip.

**Long, narrow fingers.** Either shape works, and this is where personal preference genuinely takes over. Long, narrow fingers have enough natural length that the elongating effect of almond is not needed, and they can carry coffin's flat tip without it reading as wide. This is the hand type where coffin's more architectural aesthetic is a genuine option without flattery trade-offs.

**Short nail beds.** Almond, and specifically at short-to-medium length. Short nail beds can look swamped by longer extensions, and almond at a shorter length does more flattering work than coffin at a length that overextends the hand's proportions.

**Wider hands overall (not just nail beds).** Almond again. Coffin's horizontal tip line can add visual width at the palm level as well as at the nail. Almond's pointed tip draws the eye to the fingertips rather than across the hand.

The one scenario where coffin consistently outperforms almond on flattery: dramatic length on long, proportionate fingers, where the coffin silhouette reads editorial rather than wide.

