## Lifestyle Reality Check: Which Shape Survives Your Actual Day?

Your nails type all day. They open packaging, carry bags, wash dishes, scroll phones, and attempt to peel a sticker label. The question is not which shape you love in a photo. It is which shape survives contact with your actual routine.

Almond is the more practical choice for keyboard-heavy, hands-on daily use. The rounded tip has no corners to catch on keys, and no sharp edge to snag on fabric or bag linings. Manicurist Erica De Los Santos has noted that almond's absence of corners means it does not catch on keyboards or daily objects the way square-cornered shapes do, making it the most functional of the tapered shapes for repetitive hand movement. The shape also grows out more comfortably, staying comfortable even as length increases.

Coffin at medium length is manageable for typing, and many people do it daily without issue. The flat tip is wider than almond's peak, which can mean more frequent contact with adjacent keys, and the corners remain the snagging risk throughout the nail's life. Long coffin (anything pushing into dramatic extension territory) does genuinely compromise everyday function. Not impossibly, but noticeably.

For press-on wearers, the lifestyle calculation includes how you apply and remove. Coffin press-ons that are not sized precisely will catch corners on everything, which accelerates lifting and shortens wear time regardless of adhesive quality. A properly fitted almond set will outlast a poorly fitted coffin set in active daily use almost every time.

The practical verdict: if you type extensively, work with your hands, or live an active daily life, almond is the lower-maintenance shape. If your daily demands are lighter, or if you are wearing a set for an event or a long weekend, coffin's drama is worth the slightly higher maintenance.

