## What Is the Modern Almond, and Is It Replacing Coffin in 2026?

Something is happening on NailTok that no competitor comparison article has explained clearly: the shape dominating nail content in 2026 is not quite coffin and not quite traditional almond. It sits between them, and the confusion around it is real.

Celebrity nail artists have named it "the modern almond": an elongated almond with a slightly squarer, softer tip than the classic pointed shape. According to the <a href='https://www.newbeauty.com/view/spring-nail-trends-2026' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>NewBeauty 2026 nail shape trend report</a>, this shape is specifically described as more chip- and break-resistant than either classic coffin or stiletto, because the softened tip eliminates the most vulnerable geometry of both extremes. It is long enough to look editorial. Wearable enough for daily use. And from a distance, it reads as the "that TikTok shape" that many readers cannot quite categorise as one or the other.

Simultaneously, coffin itself is evolving. The extreme long coffin (the Kylie Jenner-era dramatic talon) has given way to a shorter, more wearable version that industry professionals are tracking as "micro coffin." The classic shape has not disappeared. It has compressed toward practicality.

What this means practically: if you have been seeing a shape on your feed that you cannot identify as definitively coffin or definitively almond, it is almost certainly the modern almond. It is not replacing coffin. It is occupying a new category adjacent to both. If you want it specifically, ask for it by name: elongated almond with a soft, slightly squared tip. Most nail artists in 2026 will know exactly what you mean.

